---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// The privacy page. Required by ADR-028: opening comments past GitHub means the
// site started holding things it did not before (commenter emails, IPs at the
// comment backend, OAuth identities once those land).
//
// EVERY FACTUAL CLAIM BELOW WAS CHECKED AGAINST THE LIVE SYSTEMS ON 2026-08-21,
// not against documentation and not against the old draft:
//   - umami: the `session` table has country/region/city columns and NO ip
//     column, so "we don't store your IP" is literally true, and "country" was
//     an understatement (it derives region and city too).
//   - comentario: author_ip + author_country on cm_comments, signup_ip on
//     cm_users, ip on cm_user_sessions, and ip on cm_domain_page_views.
//   - the event list is the actual set of data-umami-event attributes in src/
//     plus the umami.track() calls in public/js/, not a summary of them. Since
//     v1.1 that includes the data-track attributes read by
//     public/js/interactions.js, which is a second mechanism for the same thing:
//     internal links use it so that tracking a click does not cancel the view
//     transition (see that file's header).
//   - "session replay is off" was read from the Umami API, not assumed:
//     recorderEnabled=false and replayConfig=null on all three websites, and the
//     script served at /pf-insights/script.js is the base tracker with no
//     replay, heatmap or mousemove code in it.
//
// RE-CHECKED 2026-09-23 for v1.5. The DB was not queried;
// these come from the running pod and the upstream source for its exact tag:
//   - comentario v3.18.0 (image tag, and the version its embed API reports).
//     Its startup log prints the effective config: Logging.FullIPs=false and
//     Stats.MaxDays=30. With FullIPs off, every write path (comments, signup,
//     login sessions, page views) passes the IP through util.MaskIP, which keeps
//     the first two IPv4 octets or IPv6 groups; the country is looked up first.
//     Sessions last 28 days (util.UserSessionDuration) and the built-in cleaner
//     deletes expired ones daily; page views are deleted after MaxDays+1 = 31.
//   - the widget sets comentario_commenter_session (SameSite=Strict, 30 days)
//     for every reader of a post, holding a fixed all-zero token until sign-in,
//     and comentario_settings (sort order) in localStorage. Seen in headless
//     Chromium against the live site, and in embed/src/api.ts.
//   - self-service deletion: the widget's Settings > "Edit Comentario profile"
//     opens the admin UI profile page, whose "Delete my account" calls
//     DELETE /api/auth/profile. The strings are in the live admin bundle.
//     Not exercised end to end: that would mean creating an account in prod.
//   - server logs: Traefik access logs for the public routers show Cloudflare
//     edge addresses as the client; the cluster's log store keeps them for
//     14 days, read from its live retention setting.
// The runbook for access and erasure requests is kept privately.
//
// If you change what is collected, change this page in the same commit, and add
// a log entry at the bottom. The old entries stay.
const version = 'v1.6';
const updated = '2026-09-24';

const log = [
  {
    version: 'v1.6',
    date: '2026-09-24',
    body: 'Added counters for selecting an article version, opening a passage comparison, and downloading an article document. They carry only the article slug and fixed version, passage, or document identifiers.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.5',
    date: '2026-09-23',
    body: 'Corrected the comments section: the comment server shortens IP addresses before saving them, and it also records browser and operating system against login sessions and page views. Added how long sessions and page views last, the cookie and local storage the comment box uses, arcade saves, the web server logs, and how to delete a comment account yourself.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.4',
    date: '2026-09-15',
    body: 'Added a support page. Clicks from it to GitHub Sponsors are counted alongside the existing outbound links.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.3',
    date: '2026-09-15',
    body: 'Added counters for links within blog prose and linked images, and for opening Chronochasm passages and sources, following reading paths, and using timeline controls. The events carry public identifiers and control settings, not the text of passages or anything typed into a form.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.2',
    date: '2026-09-10',
    body: 'Added a GitLab profile link to both site footers. Clicks on it are counted alongside the existing outbound links.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.1',
    date: '2026-08-27',
    body: 'Added interaction events. The sites counted page views and a handful of link clicks; they now also count which listing you came into a post from, the tag, related and older/newer links, and the buttons that do something without loading a new page: the arcade emulators, the photo viewer, the resume print button, the mobile menu, the skip link. All of them counters. Session replay and heatmaps stay off.',
  },
  {
    version: 'v1.0',
    date: '2026-08-21',
    body: 'First version, written when comments moved off GitHub to a self-hosted Comentario instance. Before this the site collected analytics only and had no page saying so.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Privacy · ProbablyFine" description="What this site collects, where it goes, how long it's kept, and how to get it deleted. Checked against the running systems, not assumed.">
  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">$ cat ./privacy</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-3">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      This site runs on hardware in my basement. There's no ad network, no third-party tracker,
      and nothing here gets sold or handed to anyone. That's the short version. The rest of this
      page is the specific version, because "we respect your privacy" is what everyone says right
      before the fine print.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-4">{version} · updated {updated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pb-8">
    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what this covers</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Both of my sites: probablyfine.dev and{' '}
        <a href="https://maxfieldallison.com" rel="noopener" data-umami-event="outbound-maxfieldallison" class="hover:text-[var(--color-ink)]">maxfieldallison.com</a>.
        Two sites, one person, one set of hardware, so one policy rather than two that drift apart.
        Where they differ, it says so below.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        It doesn't cover sites I link to, which have their own terms and none of my influence.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># analytics</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        I use Umami, self-hosted on my own cluster. It's served from this domain at
        <span class="font-mono text-sm">/pf-insights/</span>, not from someone else's analytics
        domain. It sets no cookies and builds no profile that follows you anywhere else.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        For each page view it records the URL, the referrer that sent you, your browser, OS and
        device type, your screen size, your language, and an approximate location worked out from
        your IP address. That location goes down to city level. The IP address itself is never
        written to the database, and there's no column in it that could hold one.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        It also records page timing numbers, the kind that tell me whether the site is slow: how
        long the largest thing on screen took to paint, how quickly it responded to your first tap,
        how much the layout jumped around. Those are measurements of my site, not of you.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        A handful of named events fire on top of plain page views. On a blog post: one when you
        reach the end, one when the "how this one was made" note scrolls into view, and one when
        you click a source link inside the article.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Elsewhere it counts the things you click that a page view would otherwise miss. The
        outbound links in the nav, footer and support page, the RSS link, the email link. Which listing you came
        into a post from, and whether you left it by a tag, by the older and newer arrows, or by
        the related list. The buttons that do something without loading a new page: starting and
        stopping a game on the arcade page, opening a photo on the pets page and moving through
        them, the print and download buttons on the resume, the navigation menu, the skip-to-content
        link. Each one is a number that goes up by one. Nothing records where on the page you
        clicked.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Links within blog prose are counted too, including which links are images. Selecting
        an article version, opening a passage comparison, or downloading a document is counted with the article slug
        and a fixed version, passage, or document identifier. The text and your reading position are not sent. On
        Chronochasm, I count opened moments and selected sources, previous and next passage
        navigation, related threads, and links out of the reader. I also count use of the
        thread filter, zoom, gap controls, moment list and motion setting. Those events carry
        the public moment, thread or source identifier and the control setting. They don't
        include the passage text, private editorial notes, or anything you type. Moving the
        pointer over the timeline and scrolling through it don't send events.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        There is no session recording, no heatmap, no mouse or scroll tracking, and nothing
        watching what you type. Umami can do session replay. It is switched off, and the script
        this site serves is the plain tracker with none of that code in it. What I get is a count
        of how many people did a thing, never a picture of one person doing it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Both sites run this the same way, as separate collections that aren't joined up. The events
        differ where the sites do: the blog, photo and arcade ones exist only on probablyfine.dev,
        and the resume ones only on maxfieldallison.com.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        If you block any of it, nothing breaks. The site doesn't check and doesn't care.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># comments</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Comments are on probablyfine.dev only. maxfieldallison.com doesn't have them, and if that
        changes this section already describes what would happen.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Comments run on Comentario, self-hosted here alongside everything else. It used to be
        Giscus, which stored threads as GitHub Discussions and meant you needed a GitHub account
        to leave a sentence. That ruled out most people who read this.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        You can comment anonymously, or make an account with an email address and a password.
        Signing in with GitHub or Google is planned and isn't live yet. When it is, this page
        changes first.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Here's what the comment server keeps. Against a comment: the text, the name you typed, and
        a shortened IP address with a country. Against an account: your email address, your display
        name, and a shortened IP address and country from when you signed up. Against a login
        session: a shortened IP address again, plus your browser and operating system. Sessions
        expire after 28 days, or when you sign out, and are deleted then. It also keeps its own
        count of views on pages that have comments, with a shortened IP address and your browser
        and operating system, and deletes each view after 31 days.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Shortened means the server keeps the first two of the four parts of an IPv4 address, like
        203.0.x.x, or the first two groups of an IPv6 one. It works out the country first and then
        drops the rest before anything is saved.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The IP logging is for spam and abuse, and that's all I use it for. I don't cross-reference
        it against the analytics, which couldn't be done anyway, because the analytics never stored
        an IP to match it to.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Avatars come from Gravatar. If you make an account, my server sends a hashed copy of your
        email address to Gravatar once, and whatever picture you've got there becomes your avatar.
        The image is then stored here and served from this domain, so your browser never talks to
        them. That hash is the one piece of anything that leaves my hardware, and if you've never
        set up a Gravatar there's nothing on the other end to find.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Comments are moderated. Anonymous ones wait for me to approve them, and so do the first few
        from a new account. That reproduces what requiring a GitHub account was doing by accident,
        without charging everyone a GitHub account to get in. It also means your comment might sit
        in a queue for a while. I'm one person.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># in your browser</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        The analytics store nothing in your browser. Two other things do, and only on the pages
        that use them.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        On a post with comments, the comment box sets a cookie called
        <span class="font-mono text-sm">comentario_commenter_session</span> and keeps your comment
        sort order in local storage. Until you sign in, the cookie holds the same placeholder value
        for everyone. Once you sign in, it holds your login session.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The arcade can keep saved games and disk changes in your browser's storage for
        arcade.probablyfine.dev. They stay on your device. The arcade server only hands out files
        and has nothing that could receive them. Clearing that site's data removes them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># server logs</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        The web servers in my cluster log each request: the address it came from, the page, the
        referrer and the browser. For visits from the internet that address is Cloudflare's rather
        than yours, because Cloudflare sits in front of the site. The logs are kept for 14 days and
        are used to find faults.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># cloudflare</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        DNS and TLS for this domain go through Cloudflare, and there's a standby copy of the site
        on Cloudflare Pages that serves you if my cluster is down. So Cloudflare sees requests to
        this domain the way it does for any site behind it. That layer runs under their privacy
        terms, not mine, and I don't control what they log.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># fonts and assets</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Everything the page loads comes from this domain. No Google Fonts, no CDN, no third-party
        scripts. The fonts are copied into the build. The one exception is described under
        comments, and it happens on my server rather than in your browser.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># getting it deleted</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Email me at{' '}
        <a href="mailto:hello@maxfieldallison.com" data-umami-event="email-click" class="hover:text-[var(--color-ink)]">hello@maxfieldallison.com</a>
        {' '}and I'll delete it. A comment, your account, everything attached to it, your choice.
        You don't have to give a reason and I won't ask for one.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        If you made an account, you can also delete it yourself. In the comment box, open Settings,
        then "Edit Comentario profile", then "Delete my account". You choose whether your comments
        go with it. If you keep them, they stay up under the name "Deleted User".
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        If you commented without an account, there's no email address to match you by. Tell me
        which post and roughly when, and I'll remove it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Same address if you think something on this page is wrong, or if you want to know what's
        stored against you before deciding.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># log</h2>
      {
        log.map((e) => (
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{e.version} · {e.date}</span> · {e.body}
          </p>
        ))
      }
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        If what's collected changes, this page changes with it and the old entry stays visible.
        It's in git, so you can check whether I've kept that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
